WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.489
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.489 --> 00:00:08.950
In computer graphics, when we are talking
about portrait relighting, we mean a technique

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:14.679
that is able to look at an image and change
the lighting, and maybe even the materials

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:18.190
or geometry after this image has been taken.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.220
This is a very challenging endeavor.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:26.630
So can neural networks put a dent into this
problem and give us something new and better?

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.630
You bet!

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.860
The examples that you see here are done with
this new work that uses a learning-based technique,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:39.890
and is able to change the lighting for human
portraits, and only requires one input image.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.380
You see, normally, using methods in computer
graphics to relight these images would require

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.690
trying to find out what the geometry of the
face, materials, and lighting is from the

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:56.450
image, and then, we can change the lighting
or other parameters, run a light simulation

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.890
program, and hope that the estimations are
good enough to make it realistic.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:05.890
However, if we wish to use neural networks
to learn the concept of portrait relighting,

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.470
of course, we need quite a bit of training
data.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:14.750
Since this is not trivially available, the
paper contains a new dataset with over 25

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.340
thousand portrait images that are relit in
5 different ways.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.350
It also proposes a neural network structure
that can learn this relighting operation efficiently.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.830
It is shaped a bit like an hourglass and contains
an encoder and decoder parts.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:35.840
The encoder part takes an image as an input
and estimates what lighting could have been

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.340
used to produce it, while the decoder part
is where we can play around with changing

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.800
the lighting, and it will generate the appropriate
image that this kind of lighting would produce.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.800
However, there is more to it.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.920
What you see here are skip connections that
are useful to save insights from different

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.290
abstraction levels and transfer them from
the encoder to the decoder network.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.420
So what does this mean exactly?

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.479
Intuitively, it is a bit like using the lighting
estimator network to teach the image generator

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:07.770
what it has learned.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:12.630
So, do we really lose a lot if we skip the
skip connections?

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.959
Well, quite a bit, have a look here.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:20.570
The image on the left shows the result using
all skip connections, while as we traverse

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.580
to the right, we see the results omitting
them.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.810
These connections indeed make a profound difference.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:32.200
Let’s be thankful for the authors of the
paper as putting together such a dataset and

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.820
trying to get an understanding as to what
network architectures it would require to

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.050
get great results like these takes quite a
bit of work.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:44.430
However, I’d like to make a note about modeling
subsurface light transport.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.630
This is a piece of footage from our earlier
paper that we wrote as a collaboration with

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.830
the Activision Blizzard company, and you can
see here that including this indeed makes

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:57.440
a profound difference in the looks of a human
face.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.470
I cannot wait to see some followup papers
that take more advanced effects like this

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.230
into consideration for relighting as well.

